{"kind":"report","text":"Approved as a documentation-only design at exact c6d416f3e6eaeb9f61c1b19ffbd97d722b190918, under request git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a2cbd459e96c2ddb55c84f34eddb76ea63d0cfca (#10286). Both exact-head artifacts were independently inspected as effective and directly on builder promise git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e635e71eca17ba273ad50729fd39b4380e887485 (#10288), with notes/2026-10-02-declared-acts.md primary.\n\nThis verdict reads the note together with the builder's effective clarification git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7307f813da979149879b9a5f357828f465df43b (#10766), which I inspected in full. The clarification resolves the two remaining ambiguous wordings without enlarging the design:\n- Legacy grants are intersected with the surviving platform vocabulary. renew remains available only if the original grant covered it; review/check-only grants acquire nothing. Expiry, revocation, grantor authority and lane restrictions still apply. The text saying only renew is not a grant of renew.\n- The v2 compatibility fixtures deliberately convert envelope bindings, recovery operations, checker configurations naming their act, and signed grant maps. Each conversion is listed for review and preserves the original assertions. The builder will make these wordings exact in the next note revision. The stage-2 phrase with bindings added is not permission to omit the other mandatory format conversions or weaken behavior.\n\nThe six findings in git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:079151998d1e28c98611e300a1240add8dcc04b5 (#10662) are resolved at design level:\n1. Section 2.3.2 binds the grantor's signed map before admission. A client expands wildcard intent before signing; later kinds are not acquired. Delegate admission, invitation admission/redemption and delegated use all check bindings. Exact retries do not re-pin grants, and old unbound grants gain no declared authority.\n2. Sections 2.3.4 and 3.9 put steps in the retained policy document. Only an admin-approved activation changes the room's engine version. Old engines remain available to admission and verify; an unsupported verifier reports its own limit. The deployment-without-activation, two-version log and accepted-retry cases cover the former unsequenced upgrade.\n3. Section 4.4 derives the required evaluation calls and rebuilds contexts from the independently folded state and version witness. Missing calls, extra decisions and substituted contexts are explicit failures before obligations/effects are derived. Section 4.3 publishes preparation before issuing a check job, establishing the generation/operation relationship a check's own witness could not supply. The forged-log cases now include omissions, false contexts and missing preparation. Git-object and clock truth limits remain explicit.\n4. Section 2.3.1 separates immutable scope source/settings from mutable current scope. Body-scoped takeover preserves today's scope replacement and overlap/generation checks; template scopes remain fixed. Section 6 preserves code-review's latest goal/plan in the record projection instead of special application fields in admission. I checked that the policy lane input contains no goal/plan field, so this projection choice does not silently remove a policy input.\n5. Section 2.3.3 makes checker configuration name exactly one declared check act, validates its role/step/body requirements and handles thread mismatch visibly with check-unroutable. The jam now declares in-key-check, supplies its require rule and configuration, and specifies the pattern-to-published-landing acceptance case.\n6. Historical opening kinds remain referenceable after retirement. The opening binding and settings remain recorded; matching by kind name across name reuse is intentional and disclosed, and distinct semantics that must not share threads use distinct names. Retirement cannot silently remove the specified existing-thread release/review path.\n\nThe earlier revision's corrections also stand: direct acts carry per-kind bindings; exact accepted retries precede stale-binding checks; thread predicates and reserved overlap enforce the jam examples; artroom-legacy-v1 is frozen with its configuration-recovery behavior; the inventory marks the R-LANE-2 implementation gap; builder owns the self-hostability judgment under git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b2cdc44a4fd2967f4ecd6a366dfa67df36f513b8 (#10289). Gate 1 remains a separate recommendation for Hugh, not an implementation cut.\n\nArchitecture: Using gitseq's layer terminology, this designs Workroom/interpreter and projection behavior (5-7), with Room host execution (4) and Git-backed evidence/landing (1); the head changes no running kernel/nexus behavior (2-3). The finite primitive set remains platform code, declarations remain data, application presentation remains a projection, and a single retained policy activation selects declared meanings and step semantics. The new prepared event is a deliberate observability cost for proving a preparation relationship, not a second authority source. No present architecture contract is changed by these two documentation files. The stage-1 protocol and type amendment must specify the contracts before runtime stages implement them.\n\nSecurity: No unresolved design defect found under the recorded clarification. Configuration changes still need admin approval and cannot judge their own authorization; recovery remains outside application declarations; grants cannot acquire changed meanings in transit; default resolution and retained steps versions bind effect semantics; check evidence remains tied to its generation, prepared integration, configuration and signer. Replay now checks completeness and reconstruction instead of merely consistency of room-selected inputs. Current roster, signature, secret-scan, lease-fencing, bounds and complete-forward landing invariants remain platform responsibilities. This documentation request expressly commissions advice only. Its approval does not satisfy the four facts needed to treat a research/advice request as adoption authority for follow-on implementation; each marked decision and implementation stage still needs the appropriate durable authorization.\n\nSimplification: The four shared rules in section 2.3 make both applications use the same mechanisms. One configuration-selected check act avoids heuristic dispatch; one steps identity avoids deploy-dependent interpretation; the signed grant map avoids a second wildcard/catalog lifecycle; goal/plan remain an application projection. Keep the complete scope. Estimates may order stages and expose risk, never remove functionality or substitute a narrower self-hostability goal.\n\nImplementation handoff points, not claims of completed runtime:\n- Stage 1 should make all new codes/events/types explicit, including check-unroutable and the verifier's unsupported-version outcome, and incorporate #10766's exact intersection and fixture wording.\n- The legacy suite must remain unchanged; the v2 fixture conversion list is reviewable as above. Added runtime guards need named red mutation evidence, not compile failures.\n- Measure the version witness and prepared-event costs once those writes actually exist. Stage 2's no-new-rows baseline and the later prepared-event exception must be reported for their respective implemented scopes, not combined into an impossible zero-cost claim.\n- The seven-act application, the jam end-to-end fixture, old recovery logs, cross-version replay and broad client/MCP/UI behavior remain mandatory acceptance scope. The note's documented primitive ceiling is a trigger for a separately designed extension when a real application needs it, not a ban on the full product ambition.\n\nEvidence and limits: Read every changed hunk in the 61,887-character revision-2-to-3 diff and its surrounding rules, together with the fully read revision 2, covering the 1,770-line current note; read the README delta. Cross-checked current RequireRule and CheckerConfig shapes, require/refuse evaluation ordering, PolicyLane inputs, delegation kind/lane restrictions and invitation redemption in the cited df22d771 sources. The new jam rule/configuration matches those retained shapes plus the proposed v2 act field. Both artifacts and checker promise were inspected individually. The new implementing commit carries the full request trailer. git diff --check passes; a merge-tree against origin/main 58a2f0a09b582b03399aed3862e4234e1c7e12b8 is clean; the detached review clone is clean.\n\nOnly the design note and README index differ from df22d771; production source, tests, manifests and lockfile are unchanged. No v2 runtime exists in this head, so there is no new runtime guard to mutate and no execution claim for the proposed fixtures. I did not rerun root test/typecheck solely for this documentation revision; the artifact's reported green gates remain builder evidence. The earlier local gitseq/dap prior-art reads are carried, not new research. No canonical source edits, live provider work, credential use or deployment occurred.\n\nThe note fulfils the nine-part documentation commission. It adopts nothing beyond git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4e4134b406fda6d8764b5d215c86db4ae5f160fa (#10285), as corrected by #10289, and commissions no build. This is approval to land the reviewed design documentation; it is not a claim that declared acts or the jam are implemented or self-hostable.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e1b239afc46d592e38c277ddb6889fcd77735bb7","binding":"assigned","head":"c6d416f3e6eaeb9f61c1b19ffbd97d722b190918","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d842d24938687f76abd7cbef72fed7110de72838\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7307f813da979149879b9a5f357828f465df43b\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e635e71eca17ba273ad50729fd39b4380e887485\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e7307f813da979149879b9a5f357828f465df43b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}